{#
compose/_link_popover.html.twig
─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
The insert-link popover, for any surface driven by `compose--compose-toolbar`.

What it replaces is `prompt('Enter URL:')` — one unthemed field, no way to say
what the link should READ as, and a bare "example.com" going in as a relative
link to a path inside the app.

ONE element with two faces rather than two popovers. They are the same object
at two moments — "what is this link" and "what should this link be" — and
Change is a switch between them rather than a different window. The controller
drives both; it is also what holds the saved selection the URL is applied to,
which is why focusing a field in here does not lose the user's place. See the
note on #savedRange in compose_toolbar_controller.js.

A partial rather than markup in the compose window, because the settings
signature editor runs the SAME controller and had the same link button — and
therefore had the same prompt. One popover, two hosts, no second copy to
forget when one of them changes.

The POSITIONING is the caller's, passed in as `class`: the compose window
anchors it above a toolbar block that spans the window and never scrolls (the
button itself lives in a sideways scroller and would drag the popover off
screen with it); the signature editor anchors it under its own small bar.

The ENTRANCE is the caller's too, and for exactly the same reason as the
positioning: a popover should travel away from the edge it is pinned to, and
the two hosts pin it to opposite ones. The compose window hangs it above the
toolbar and passes `rise`; the signature editor drops it under its own bar and
takes the default, which is the direction-neutral fade rather than a guess that
would be wrong half the time.

Only the popover itself is marked. The two faces below toggle `hidden` as well,
and marking them would play an entrance every time Change flips between them —
which is one object changing what it says, not a second one arriving.

Variables:
  id     string  unique suffix for the two field ids
  class  string  positioning and width classes for the popover box
  enter  string  motion.css entrance name (default 'fade')
#}
<div data-compose--compose-toolbar-target="linkPopover" hidden
     data-enter="{{ enter|default('fade') }}"
     role="dialog"
     aria-label="{{ 'compose.link.dialog'|trans }}"
     data-action="keydown->compose--compose-toolbar#linkKeydown"
     class="{{ class|default('absolute bottom-full mb-2 left-2 right-2 md:left-auto md:right-3 md:w-80') }}
            z-40 popover p-3 text-sm">

    {# ── Editing face ──────────────────────────────────────────── #}
    <div data-compose--compose-toolbar-target="linkEdit" class="space-y-2">
        <p data-compose--compose-toolbar-target="linkHeading"
           class="text-[10px] font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-ink-faint">
            {{ 'compose.link.add'|trans }}
        </p>

        <div>
            <label for="link-url-{{ id }}" class="block text-[11px] text-ink-faint mb-0.5">
                {{ 'compose.link.url_label'|trans }}
            </label>
            {# `text`, not `url`: a bare "example.com" is what people type and
               is exactly what the controller normalises to https://example.com.
               type=url would have the browser refuse it before we saw it. #}
            <input type="text" inputmode="url"
                   id="link-url-{{ id }}"
                   data-compose--compose-toolbar-target="linkUrl"
                   autocomplete="off" spellcheck="false"
                   placeholder="{{ 'compose.link.url_placeholder'|trans }}"
                   class="w-full rounded-lg border border-line bg-surface px-2 py-1.5
                          text-sm text-ink placeholder:text-ink-faint outline-none
                          focus:ring-2 focus:ring-accent">
        </div>

        <div>
            <label for="link-text-{{ id }}" class="block text-[11px] text-ink-faint mb-0.5">
                {{ 'compose.link.text_label'|trans }}
            </label>
            <input type="text"
                   id="link-text-{{ id }}"
                   data-compose--compose-toolbar-target="linkText"
                   autocomplete="off"
                   placeholder="{{ 'compose.link.text_placeholder'|trans }}"
                   class="w-full rounded-lg border border-line bg-surface px-2 py-1.5
                          text-sm text-ink placeholder:text-ink-faint outline-none
                          focus:ring-2 focus:ring-accent">
        </div>

        <p data-compose--compose-toolbar-target="linkError" hidden
           role="alert" class="text-xs text-danger"></p>

        <div class="flex items-center gap-2 pt-0.5">
            <button type="button"
                    data-action="click->compose--compose-toolbar#applyLink"
                    class="rounded-full bg-accent text-white text-sm font-semibold
                           px-3 py-1.5 hover:bg-white/10 transition-colors">
                {{ 'compose.link.apply'|trans }}
            </button>
            <button type="button"
                    data-action="click->compose--compose-toolbar#closeLink"
                    class="rounded-full px-3 py-1.5 text-sm text-ink-muted
                           hover:bg-hover hover:text-ink transition-colors">
                {{ 'compose.link.cancel'|trans }}
            </button>
            {# Only while an existing link is being edited — there is nothing
               to unlink otherwise. #}
            <button type="button" hidden
                    data-compose--compose-toolbar-target="linkUnlink"
                    data-action="click->compose--compose-toolbar#removeLink"
                    class="ml-auto text-xs text-danger hover:underline">
                {{ 'compose.link.remove'|trans }}
            </button>
        </div>
    </div>

    {# ── Viewing face ──────────────────────────────────────────────
       A click in a contenteditable only places a caret, so a link the user
       just wrote is otherwise unreachable without sending the message first.
       This is the Gmail affordance: the address, and the three things you can
       do to it. #}
    <div data-compose--compose-toolbar-target="linkView" hidden class="space-y-2">
        <p class="text-[10px] font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-ink-faint">
            {{ 'compose.link.heading'|trans }}
        </p>
        {# A real anchor, so the browser's own "copy link address" and
           middle-click work on it. #}
        <a data-compose--compose-toolbar-target="linkHref"
           href="#" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"
           class="block truncate text-sm text-accent hover:underline"></a>
        <div class="flex items-center gap-1">
            <button type="button"
                    data-action="click->compose--compose-toolbar#openLink"
                    class="px-2 py-1 rounded text-sm text-ink-muted hover:bg-hover
                           hover:text-ink transition-colors">
                {{ 'compose.link.open'|trans }}
            </button>
            <button type="button"
                    data-action="click->compose--compose-toolbar#changeLink"
                    class="px-2 py-1 rounded text-sm text-ink-muted hover:bg-hover
                           hover:text-ink transition-colors">
                {{ 'compose.link.change'|trans }}
            </button>
            <button type="button"
                    data-action="click->compose--compose-toolbar#removeLink"
                    class="px-2 py-1 rounded text-sm text-danger hover:bg-hover
                           transition-colors">
                {{ 'compose.link.remove'|trans }}
            </button>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>
